WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.560
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome Tom Hartmann
back to the program, Tom hosts the Tom Hartman

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.160
program and is also author of The Hidden History
Series.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.520
The latest book in the series is The Hidden
History of Big Brother in America, How the

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.600
Death of Privacy and the Rise of Surveillance
Threaten US and our democracy.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.820
Tom, always great speaking to you.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.220
Speaker 5: Hey, David, great to be here with
you.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.519
Thank you for inviting me.

00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:26.960
Speaker 1: So James Gleich wrote this book,
The Information, and one of the things that

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:32.309
Gleich writes in the book is that for as long
as there has been any communication technology,

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:37.649
think drums to communicate over long distances
in the African savannah.

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:44.850
There has also been the ability to surveil,
intercept, send confusing messages to trick

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:49.039
those wishing to communicate in that in that
method, and you can sort of follow that with

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:52.860
every communications technology up through
today.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:58.539
When and how would you say that what we might
consider the modern kind of surveillance and

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:02.489
lack of privacy apparatus really got going
in the United States?

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:11.030
Speaker 5: It was in the late 90s, prior to
1996, the internet operated under the same

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:17.210
telecommunications rules as telephone companies,
and this went back to the 1930s.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.590
To this day, if you're on a phone call with
somebody and it's actually being carried over

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.750
cell towers, it's not a VoIP call.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:27.460
It's not a voice over internet call because
the rules for the internet are now different.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:31.720
But if you're talking to somebody on a cell
tower or on a regular phone for the phone

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.200
company to listen in, for the government to
listen in, for any third party to listen in,

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.820
they have to have a warrant.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.860
There has to be a legal basis for that intrusion.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:47.700
In 1996, with the Telecommunications Act,
Section 230 said, we're going to drop that

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.190
for the internet so that that's one piece
of it.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:58.630
The other is another metaphor that to kind
of explain where we're at is that prior to

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:04.710
1996, the rules for people who host sites
on the internet were very similar to the rules.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:11.840
For people who own disco clubs, you know,
dance venues, bars, restaurants or you're

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:12.840
in your home.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.260
I mean, if you, David, were to put a sign
out in front of your house that said, Hey,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.341
big party on Saturday, come on and just leave
the front door open and you're sitting in

00:02:20.341 --> 00:02:21.680
your living room Saturday afternoon.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:22.920
The sun's going down.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.700
There's there's somebody raping a woman in
the back room.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.380
There's a there's a bunch of people shooting
up heroin over in the corner and there's a

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:36.450
guy in the other back room who is who is selling
illegal pornography and the police bust in.

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.730
They would not only arrest all those people,
they would arrest you as the host of the party,

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:41.730
right?

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.800
You have a responsibility for what happens
in your house that you know about.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.170
And that was, you know, I mean, this goes
back to British Common Law back to literally

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:50.850
the 10th century.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:57.760
So Section 230 of the Telecommunications Act
of 1996 said that's still true for your house.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.390
It's still true for a bar.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:00.690
It's still true for a restaurant.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.650
Is still true for a civic center.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:06.790
But it's not true for anybody who builds a
house on the internet.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:11.450
And thus, Mark Zuckerberg became one of the
wealthiest men in the world.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:14.569
He built a house and he said, Hey, come on
in and have a party.

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:18.550
And he largely ignored the people who were
shooting heroin in the corner and selling,

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.069
you know, I mean, they started.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:21.770
They eventually started cracking down.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:25.569
Obviously, they were from the beginning and
on the obvious stuff.

00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:30.190
But even now, there's just I mean, you know,
this new report, the January six, was largely

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:31.190
planned on Facebook.

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:32.459
And this isn't just a trash Facebook.

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:36.440
I'm not, you know, he's playing by the rules.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:37.440
And that's the problem.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:43.540
I mean, you know, when you have capitalism
in a republic, the job of government is to

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:49.300
set the rules so that capitalism can work
in a way that doesn't damage society.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.510
And we have failed in that regard.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.510
And so the
Speaker 1: Republicans during the Trump era

